/// Headscale's control-plane API must be publicly reachable (roaming clients need to reach it
/// before any tunnel exists), so it gets its own public hostname. Headplane's admin UI shares that
/// same hostname, under the "/admin" path, but stays LAN/tailnet-only via `headplaneAllowListMiddleware`
/// below, since it's the higher-value target of the two.
///
/// Headplane's `config_path` mounts Headscale's own ConfigMap read-only, so it can display (but
/// not edit) `config.yaml` itself. Editing that would need `shareProcessNamespace` plus pod-read
/// RBAC so Headplane can SIGHUP-reload Headscale, and Headscale's SIGHUP handler only reloads the
/// ACL policy anyway, not the rest of config.yaml, so that complexity isn't worth it here.
///
/// ACL policy is handled separately from `config.yaml`: `policy.mode: database` makes Headscale
/// store it in its own database instead of a file, which is what lets Headplane's ACL editor read
/// and write it live over Headscale's API (`GET`/`PUT /v1/policy`), no reload needed. Tradeoff:
/// the policy then lives outside this repo, in Headscale's sqlite DB, rather than being
/// GitOps-tracked here.
///
/// Headplane's cookie-signing secret is fetched from OpenBao via `headplaneSecretsExternalSecret`
/// below, seeded once with (see `../openbao/app.pkl`'s bootstrap step 5):
///
/// bao kv put secret/headplane cookie-secret="$(openssl rand -hex 16)"
///
/// Must be exactly 32 characters (Headplane's own schema rejects anything else), hence
/// `-hex 16` (16 bytes = 32 hex chars).
///
/// To log into Headplane, generate a Headscale API key and use it as the login credential:
///
/// kubectl exec -n headscale deploy/headscale -- headscale apikeys create
///
/// The subnet router advertising the home VLANs (and exit node) into the tailnet runs as the
/// `tailscale-unifi` addon natively on the UniFi Cloud Gateway itself, not tracked in this repo.
/// See <https://github.com/unifi-utilities/unifi-common-addons/tree/main/tailscale-unifi> for setup.
///
/// By default the UniFi Cloud Gateway's own DNS resolver doesn't listen on the tailscale interface,
/// so it never answers queries routed to it by `dns.nameservers.split` (see
/// `manifests/headscale/config-map.pkl`), even though the gateway is otherwise reachable over the
/// tailnet (confirmed via `dig @<router-ip>` timing out while a plain HTTPS request to the same IP
/// succeeded). Fixed per <https://github.com/SierraSoftworks/tailscale-unifi/issues/122>. Required
/// for split DNS to work at all, not just an optimization.
module chart

import ".../utils/ExternalSecret.pkl"
import "app.pkl"

headplaneSecretsExternalSecret =
  new ExternalSecret {
    name = app.headplaneSecretName
    secrets {
      ["headplane"] {
        "cookie-secret"
      }
    }
  }.resource

output {
  value = new Listing {
    headplaneSecretsExternalSecret
    for (_, resource in import*("manifests/**.pkl")) {
      resource
    }
  }
  renderer = new YamlRenderer { isStream = true }
}
